does (?i) fold the BACKREFERENCE comparison too?
(?i)(a)\1    vs 'a' + U+0041            yes
(?i)(k)\1    vs 'k' + U+212A            yes

one program, two answers about the same pair
  str.casefold: U+00DF == 'ss'          True    re (?i): no
  str.casefold: U+212A == 'k'           True    re (?i): yes
  str.casefold: U+017F == 's'           True    re (?i): yes
  str.lower(U+1E9E) == U+00DF           True    re (?i): yes
  str.upper(U+00DF) is 2 code points    True    re (?i): no

normalise first, then fold -- what the advice at the end of the page does
  NFKC(U+212A) = U+004B         and casefolded = U+006B
  NFKC(U+017F) = U+0073         and casefolded = U+0073
  NFKC(U+00DF) = U+00DF         and casefolded = U+0073 U+0073
  NFKC(U+1E9E) = U+1E9E         and casefolded = U+0073 U+0073
